    "text": "The dispatch office hummed with the low, constant breath of the building\u2019s heating system. It was a sound Aris had long stopped hearing, just as he no longer noticed the faint, metallic tang of ozone from the old relay boards or the way the fluorescent lights above his desk cast a sickly, greenish pallor on his hands. 2:17 AM. The dead hour. The time when the rails were quietest, the freight schedules thinned to a trickle, and the silence in the room felt less like peace and more like a held breath.\n\nHe was logging a delay for a grain train outside of Fargo when his pen ran dry. Cursing under his breath, he rattled the desk drawer for a spare. It was a graveyard of forgotten things: dried-up glue sticks, a single chopstick, a tarnished key to a locker he no longer used. His fingers brushed against the worn, cloth-bound spine of a book. He pulled it out.\n\nIt was a timetable from 1988. The cover was a faded, sun-bleached blue, the gold lettering almost completely rubbed away. He only kept it for the old paper smell, a ghost of the world he\u2019d entered as a young man. He flipped it open, the pages brittle and yellowed, and a small, folded piece of paper, no bigger than a playing card, fluttered out onto his desk.\n\nIt wasn't the crisp white of modern paper, but a soft, aged cream, the kind you\u2019d find in a stationery set. The",
